---
import LessonLayout    from '@/layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import LessonHeader    from '@/components/LessonHeader.astro';
import LessonCallout   from '@/components/LessonCallout.astro';
import LessonQuiz      from '@/components/LessonQuiz.astro';
import DeepDive        from '@/components/DeepDive.astro';
import LessonMindMap   from '@/components/LessonMindMap.astro';

const mindMap = `
# Real-World Applications and Business Value
## Three Production Domains
### Customer service and support
### Software development
### Research and analysis
## Four Value Drivers
### Cost reduction at scale
### Speed and 24/7 operation
### Consistency no fatigue
### Scalability without hiring
## Market Adoption Numbers
### 79% orgs using agents (PwC 2025)
### 40% enterprise apps by 2026
### $450B+ market by 2035
## Enterprise Case Studies
### IBM: 94% HR tasks automated
### McKinsey: 40-50% faster delivery
### Unity: 8000 tickets deflected
## Tensions
### Speed of automation vs Workforce impact
### Published ROI vs Survivorship bias
### Broad capabilities vs Domain specificity
## Pitfalls
### Conflating ReAct agent with simple chatbot
### Treating cost figures as guaranteed ROI
### Skipping automatable-task analysis
`;
---

<LessonLayout
  title="L08 — Real-World Applications & Business Value"
>

  <LessonHeader
    num="L08"
    title="Real-World Applications & Business Value"
    subtitle="Three domains where agents already work in production, four value drivers, and the market numbers every stakeholder asks about."
  />

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <p>
      You've built agents. Now it's time to zoom out and see where they're already running in
      production — and why organisations are willing to invest heavily in them. This lesson is
      the "why bother?" answer you'll use when pitching a project or deciding whether an agent
      is the right tool for a business problem.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Three Domains Where Agents Deliver Today</h2>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Domain</th>
            <th>What agents do</th>
            <th>Real examples</th>
            <th>Headline result</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Customer Service &amp; Support</strong></td>
            <td>Handle inquiries, troubleshoot via knowledge-base RAG, create tickets, escalate edge cases to humans</td>
            <td>Zendesk AI, Ada, Salesforce Einstein Copilot</td>
            <td>40–60% cost reduction; Unity deflected 8,000 tickets saving $1.3 M</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Software Development</strong></td>
            <td>Code generation, bug fixing, test creation, documentation, autonomous PR submission</td>
            <td>GitHub Copilot (agent mode), Claude Code</td>
            <td>Significant productivity uplift; handles features end-to-end with git workflows</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Research &amp; Analysis</strong></td>
            <td>Multi-source web search, information extraction, synthesis, structured report generation</td>
            <td>Deep Research (ChatGPT / Claude / Gemini), STORM (Stanford)</td>
            <td>Hours of research compressed to minutes with consistent quality</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <LessonCallout variant="info" title="Not an exhaustive list">
      <p>
        Agents are also being deployed in finance (fraud detection, trading), healthcare
        (clinical note summarisation, patient triage), legal (contract review), and HR
        (AskHR-style automation). The three domains above have the most mature, public
        case study evidence.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Four Core Value Drivers</h2>

    <p>When stakeholders ask "what's the ROI?", map the answer to these four levers:</p>

    <div class="vd-grid">
      <div class="vd-card">
        <div class="vd-icon">💰</div>
        <div class="vd-label">Cost Reduction</div>
        <p class="vd-body">
          Automate repetitive tasks. Scale without proportional headcount growth.
          A single customer-service agent can handle thousands of queries that would otherwise
          require a team.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="vd-card">
        <div class="vd-icon">⚡</div>
        <div class="vd-label">Speed &amp; Efficiency</div>
        <p class="vd-body">
          24/7 operation, instant responses, parallel task execution.
          Research that takes a human analyst days can complete in minutes.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="vd-card">
        <div class="vd-icon">✅</div>
        <div class="vd-label">Consistency &amp; Quality</div>
        <p class="vd-body">
          No fatigue, no Monday-morning errors. Agents follow the same checklist
          every time — code reviews, compliance checks, and QA gates become uniform.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="vd-card">
        <div class="vd-icon">📈</div>
        <div class="vd-label">Scalability</div>
        <p class="vd-body">
          Handle a 10× traffic spike with no hiring or training lag.
          Workloads that would require spinning up a team can be absorbed instantly.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Market Adoption Reality</h2>

    <p>These aren't projections for a distant future — adoption is already well underway:</p>

    <div class="stat-grid">
      <div class="stat-card">
        <div class="stat-num">79%</div>
        <div class="stat-desc">of organisations report AI agents already in use <span class="stat-src">(PwC, 2025)</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat-card">
        <div class="stat-num">78%</div>
        <div class="stat-desc">using AI in at least one business function — up from 72% the year before <span class="stat-src">(McKinsey, 2024)</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat-card">
        <div class="stat-num">40%</div>
        <div class="stat-desc">of enterprise apps will feature task-specific agents by 2026 (from &lt;5% in 2025) <span class="stat-src">(Gartner, 2025)</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat-card">
        <div class="stat-num">$450B+</div>
        <div class="stat-desc">market size projected by 2035 — 30% of enterprise software revenue <span class="stat-src">(Gartner, 2025)</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Measured Returns</h3>

    <p>Among organisations that have already adopted agents (PwC survey):</p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Outcome</th><th>% of adopters reporting it</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Measurable productivity gains</td><td>66%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cost savings</td><td>57%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Faster decision-making</td><td>55%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Improved customer experience</td><td>54%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Enterprise Case Studies</h2>

    <div class="cs-grid">
      <div class="cs-card">
        <div class="cs-logo">IBM</div>
        <ul class="cs-facts">
          <li><strong>$3.5 B</strong> in productivity savings by 2024 (targeting $4.5 B by end of 2025)</li>
          <li><strong>11.5 M+</strong> interactions handled by the AskHR agent</li>
          <li><strong>94%</strong> of routine HR tasks automated</li>
          <li><strong>70%</strong> of customer inquiries resolved by digital assistants</li>
          <li><strong>$600 M</strong> in enterprise IT cost savings since 2022</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-card">
        <div class="cs-logo">McKinsey clients</div>
        <ul class="cs-facts">
          <li><strong>40–50%</strong> faster delivery timelines on modernisation projects</li>
          <li><strong>&gt;40%</strong> cost reduction in software modernisation</li>
          <li>Bank AI agent factory cut IT backlog time <strong>and</strong> labour by <strong>50%+</strong></li>
          <li>$450 B–$650 B additional annual revenue potential by 2030 (McKinsey estimate)</li>
          <li>30–50% cost savings from automating repetitive knowledge work</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <LessonCallout variant="key" title="The window is right now">
      <p>
        Adoption crossed the majority threshold in 2025. The organisations moving fast aren't
        waiting for the technology to mature — they're learning by doing and compounding the
        advantage. The skills you're building in this course put you directly in that cohort.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <LessonQuiz questions={[
      {
        q:       'Which business value driver best explains "a support agent handling a 10x traffic spike without degradation"?',
        options: ['Cost Reduction', 'Speed & Efficiency', 'Consistency & Quality', 'Scalability'],
        answer:  3,
        explain: 'Absorbing sudden workload spikes with no hiring delay is the definition of Scalability — handle variable demand without resource constraints.',
      },
      {
        q:       'According to Gartner, what percentage of enterprise apps featured task-specific AI agents in 2025?',
        options: ['Under 5%', '33%', '40%', '79%'],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'The Gartner figure is <5% in 2025, projected to grow to 40% by 2026. The rapid jump is the key insight — early movers have a compounding advantage.',
      },
      {
        q:       "IBM's AskHR agent automates what share of routine HR tasks?",
        options: ['57%', '70%', '79%', '94%'],
        answer:  3,
        explain: '94% of routine HR tasks are automated — a near-complete handoff for well-defined workflows, with humans handling the remaining edge cases.',
      },
    ]} />

    <DeepDive
      sources="§1.12"
      tensions={[
        { a: 'Speed of automation', b: 'Change management and workforce impact' },
        { a: 'Public case-study ROI figures', b: 'Survivorship bias — failed projects rarely publish results' },
        { a: 'Broad agent capabilities', b: 'Domain specificity needed for reliable production use' },
      ]}
      questions={[
        {
          q:      'A stakeholder shows you these statistics and says "we should automate everything". What is the most important question to ask before agreeing?',
          hint:   'Think about the conditions under which these case studies succeeded.',
          answer: '<p>Ask: <strong>Which specific tasks are well-defined enough for an agent to handle reliably?</strong> The headline numbers come from bounded, high-volume workflows (HR FAQs, order status queries, code review checklists). Broad automation without that boundary analysis leads to costly failures and erodes trust in the technology. The right answer is "let\'s map our task landscape first, then identify the highest-value, highest-confidence candidates."</p>',
        },
        {
          q:      'The IBM and McKinsey results look impressive. What should make an engineer cautious about treating them as benchmarks for a new project?',
          hint:   'Consider what you do and do not know about how those organisations got there.',
          answer: '<p>Survivorship and selection bias: IBM and McKinsey publish successes. The failed deployments, the projects that were quietly sunset, and the partial rollbacks don\'t make the press release. Enterprise results also reflect years of iteration, significant investment in data quality and change management, and domain-specific fine-tuning — none of which shows up in a single stat. Treat them as proof of what\'s <em>possible</em>, not a prediction for your first project.</p>',
        },
      ]}
      pitfalls={[
        'Quoting adoption statistics without clarifying whether "AI agent" means a ReAct loop or a simple chatbot — the definition varies wildly across surveys.',
        'Treating cost-reduction figures as guaranteed ROI without accounting for implementation cost, maintenance, and the long tail of edge-case handling.',
        'Skipping the "is this task actually automatable?" analysis and jumping straight to "agents will handle it" based on market momentum.',
      ]}
    />

  </div>

  <LessonMindMap content={mindMap} />

</LessonLayout>

<style>
  .vd-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }
  .vd-card {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 1.25rem;
    background: #f8fafc;
  }
  .vd-icon { font-size: 1.6rem; margin-bottom: .5rem; }
  .vd-label { font-weight: 700; font-size: .9rem; margin-bottom: .5rem; }
  .vd-body { font-size: .82rem; color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.55; margin: 0; }

  .stat-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(180px, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }
  .stat-card {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 1.25rem;
    background: #1a1a2e;
    color: #fff;
    text-align: center;
  }
  .stat-num {
    font-size: 2rem;
    font-weight: 900;
    color: #a5b4fc;
    line-height: 1;
    margin-bottom: .5rem;
  }
  .stat-desc { font-size: .78rem; color: #cbd5e1; line-height: 1.5; }
  .stat-src  { display: block; font-size: .7rem; color: #64748b; margin-top: .3rem; }

  .cs-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }
  .cs-card {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 1.25rem;
  }
  .cs-logo {
    font-size: .75rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: .05em;
    color: var(--accent);
    margin-bottom: .75rem;
    padding-bottom: .5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .cs-facts {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
    font-size: .83rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }
  .cs-facts li { margin-bottom: .3rem; }
  .cs-facts strong { color: var(--text); }
</style>
